"""Study guidance for the routing-focused saved exam (source keys remain unchanged)."""
def annotate(questions, notes):
    topics = [
      (['administrative distance','trustworthiness','[1/0]'], 'Administrative distance compares how much a router trusts different route sources for the same prefix. Lower wins: directly connected 0, ordinary static 1, internal EIGRP 90, OSPF 110, and RIP 120. In [110/65], 110 is distance and 65 is the protocol metric. A configured trailing distance overrides the static default.', 'Compare only the choices actually supplied. A floating backup needs a higher distance than the primary route, not a lower one.'),
      (['longest','best match','destination ip','destination address','exit interface to forward','packet destined'], 'Forwarding uses the destination IP address and the longest matching prefix. A /27 covers 32 addresses: .192–.223 and .224–.255 are different ranges. Find the matching network, then read that route’s outgoing interface or resolve its next hop. A more specific match beats a less specific one even when the less specific route has a lower administrative distance.', 'For example, .206 is in .192/27; .244 is in .224/27. Do not select a route based on the packet’s source address.'),
      (['mac address','addressing that','destination field'], 'For a remote destination, the IP packet identifies the final destination host while the Ethernet frame identifies the next hop on the current link. The sender uses its default gateway’s MAC when the destination is outside its subnet. At the next routed hop, the router builds a new link-layer frame. ARP resolves an IPv4 next hop’s MAC on a local Ethernet link.', 'Keep packet destination and frame destination separate. A gateway MAC does not mean the gateway replaces the final destination IP.'),
      (['default route','default static','last resort','::/0','0.0.0.0'], 'A default route matches destinations that have no more specific route. IPv4 uses ip route 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 followed by a reachable next hop or suitable exit interface. IPv6 uses ipv6 route ::/0. The zero-length prefix means no destination bits must match. Edge and stub routers commonly send unknown traffic toward their upstream router.', 'A default is a fallback, not a replacement for all specific entries. A /24 is a specific network, whereas /0 is the default.'),
      (['switching','cef','forwarding mechanism'], 'Process switching handles packets through the CPU routing process. Fast switching uses a route cache: the first packet establishes cached forwarding information and later packets can reuse it. Cisco Express Forwarding uses the forwarding information base (FIB) and adjacency information, rather than the demand-built fast-switching route cache.', 'Remember process → CPU, fast → cache, CEF → FIB and adjacency. Calling CEF a route-cache method confuses these mechanisms.'),
      (['ipv6','link-local'], 'Enable IPv6 forwarding with ipv6 unicast-routing in global configuration mode. An IPv6 static route specifies a destination prefix and a next hop and/or outgoing interface. A link-local next hop is meaningful only on its local link, so specify its exit interface to remove ambiguity. Verify configured static entries with show ipv6 route static.', 'Do not put ipv6 unicast-routing under an interface. Identical link-local addresses can exist on different links; the outgoing interface identifies which neighbor is intended.'),
      (['next-hop','next hop','fully specified','full specified','exit-interface'], 'Distinguish the three static-route forms. A next-hop route gives the adjacent router’s IP; an exit-interface route gives only the outgoing interface; a fully specified route supplies both. A next-hop address must be reachable. On Ethernet, naming both can avoid ambiguity and unnecessary address resolution for many remote destinations.', 'An exit-interface-only static route is generally suited to point-to-point links. To change a route, remove the old exact route and add the replacement; those are two different commands.'),
      (['dynamic routing','static routing','routing protocol','remote network','floating'], 'Static routes are configured by an administrator; dynamic protocols exchange messages, maintain routing data structures, and run algorithms to learn paths. Static routing has less update overhead and fits stable stub networks. Dynamic routing adapts to topology changes and scales better as networks grow. A floating static route acts as a backup when its primary route disappears.', 'A route must have a usable next hop or interface to install. Configured text alone does not prove the route is active in the routing table.'),
      (['child route','parent route','identifies','routing table entry','show ip route','directly connected','directly-connected'], 'Read route fields separately: C means a directly connected network, L means the router’s own local address, S means static, O means OSPF, and * marks a candidate default. The destination prefix, [distance/metric], next hop, elapsed time, and exit interface each describe a different property. An addressed interface must be operational before its connected route appears.', 'Use show ip route to inspect IPv4 routing entries and show ip interface brief to compare addressing with interface status. An IPv4 local host route normally uses /32.'),
      (['decision process','forwarding function'], 'Follow the sequence used by this reviewer: a frame arrives at the ingress interface; the router examines the destination IP and routing table; it selects the longest matching prefix; then it encapsulates the packet for the outgoing link and sends it through the egress interface.', 'Ingress means incoming and egress means outgoing. Routing chooses a path; forwarding performs the per-packet work of sending it along that path.'),
      (['hostname','password','console','login'], 'The hostname command changes a router’s name in global configuration. For a console line password, configure the password under line console 0 and enable checking with login. The service password-encryption command obscures supported plaintext passwords in the configuration; it does not turn a Telnet session into an encrypted connection.', 'Command context matters. Global configuration, interface configuration and line configuration accept different commands. Password obfuscation is not strong protection for a configuration file.'),
      (['ping','telnet','ipconfig','troubleshoot'], 'Work from observable evidence. A successful ping establishes basic IP reachability but does not prove that a particular application or transport service works. Inspect service configuration, transport reachability and filtering when an application still fails. Use ipconfig /renew to request a DHCP lease and ping with a destination address to test basic reachability.', 'Choose a command for the property being tested: interface state, IP routes, DHCP configuration and application access are distinct checks.'),
    ]
    for q in questions:
        t=q['question'].lower()
        matched=next(((why,tip) for keys,why,tip in topics if any(k in t for k in keys)),None)
        if matched:
            why,tip=matched
            notes[str(q['id'])]='Source answer: '+ ' · '.join(q['correctAnswers'])+'.\n\nWhy / how to work it out: '+why+'\n\nMemory cue & common mix-up: '+tip
        if q['id']==32:
            q['answerKeyWarning']='The saved review labels this False. The wording is ambiguous: fast switching is also an older mechanism. The expected course term here appears to be process switching.'
            notes[str(q['id'])]+='\n\nSource-key caution: '+q['answerKeyWarning']
